1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,860
name. Amen.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,980
Thank you.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:26,500
Well, good morning.

4
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:29,160
Happy Easter, everyone.

5
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:32,220
Thank you so much for joining us.

6
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:36,760
We are seriously so privileged and honored that you would come celebrate the day of

7
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:38,680
our Lord's resurrection this morning.

8
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:41,480
This is the pinnacle of the Christian faith.

9
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:42,560
This day.

10
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:45,320
Everything else hangs on this day.

11
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:51,440
This is our third week in a series we've called A Living Savior because Christians, especially

12
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:54,600
in our culture, for whatever reason, like we gravitate towards Christmas.

13
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:56,840
We spend like weeks thinking about Christmas.

14
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:58,940
And I don't know about you, but Easter kind of sneaks up on me.

15
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:03,160
And also as Christians, we walk around with crosses all over the place, right?

16
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:04,360
The cross is the symbol.

17
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:08,100
And rightly so, because on the cross, Jesus said, it is finished.

18
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:12,040
But again, apart from the resurrection, all of that falls apart.

19
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:13,980
And so today is the day.

20
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:17,160
It's the pinnacle of everything that we believe as Christians.

21
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:23,560
And so we want to look at, what does it look like for us to submit to a risen Savior who

22
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:26,040
accomplishes victory on the other side of death?

23
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:34,260
Corey talked last week, the Jews were expecting a Messiah who was going to march into Jerusalem

24
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:35,640
and save them with power.

25
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:39,700
And instead, Jesus goes silently like a lamb to be slain.

26
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:43,120
And so as Christians, this is our Messiah.

27
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:44,560
This is the one that we worship.

28
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:48,560
We look to a God who accomplishes victory after death.

29
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,780
We sit in that tension.

30
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:57,420
Today, you might have guessed, we're going to turn our eyes to the three most important days

31
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:03,940
in human history, Good Friday, Silent Saturday, and Resurrection Sunday.

32
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:09,020
And depending on where you come from, what your background might be, this whole resurrection

33
00:03:09,020 --> 00:03:11,420
thing might be a little bit iffy to you.

34
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:17,140
It might just be a hope story for people who are weak-minded, who just want to believe.

35
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:23,880
But I want us to really grapple with the reality of the fact that one-fourth of the human population

36
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:26,840
professes to believe in the resurrection of Jesus.

37
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:31,480
This is not some fairy tale that just a few people grab onto with hope.

38
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:36,200
This is based in history, and there are facts and archaeology.

39
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:41,340
There are undeniable and unignorable evidences that you have to at least look into.

40
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:49,240
You don't have to agree with the resurrection, but ignoring it, pretending like it's some fairy tale,

41
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:50,420
is not an option.

42
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:56,280
There's a secular historian by the name of H.G. Wells, and he says this,

43
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:57,820
I am a historian.

44
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:04,880
I am not a believer, but I must confess as a historian that the penniless preacher from Nazareth

45
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:07,920
is irrevocably the very center of history.

46
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:13,700
Jesus Christ is easily the most dominant figure in all of history.

47
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:19,560
So wherever you come into this room, ignoring that fact is not an option.

48
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:25,380
This man claimed to be God and claimed to offer salvation and eternal life and hope for the future,

49
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:27,360
and you've got to do something with that.

50
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:29,760
Sound good?

51
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:30,880
All right.

52
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:32,520
Don't worry, though.

53
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:34,560
Today is not going to be an apologetics class.

54
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:38,000
If it was, I would not be the one up here teaching it.

55
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,540
Today is about the hope that the resurrection brings.

56
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:50,340
So if the biblical accounts are true, if these three days happened the way that the Bible explains that they happened,

57
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:53,260
they are the most important three days in human history.

58
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:58,240
And so how do these three days impact the earliest followers of Jesus?

59
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:00,240
We like to go back in context.

60
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:02,060
That's what we do here at Canvas.

61
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:06,340
We like to look at the earliest, at how the writings affected the earliest readers,

62
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:08,680
and then draw those applications on us.

63
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:11,720
So how might their stories overlap with our stories?

64
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:15,140
And what impact might these three days have on your life and mine?

65
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:15,900
Sound good?

66
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:18,360
Okay, let's pray.

67
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:22,860
Father, Abba,

68
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:39,560
We thank you for preserving the gospel accounts for us, for recording for us a narrative that has tension and struggle and drama and humanity all over it.

69
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:57,960
Because you came for us and you came for us and we are messy and we are broken and we mess things up and we fail to believe and we doubt and we sin and we rebel and the Scriptures are littered with all of those things because that's the very same people you came to redeem.

70
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:06,740
So, Father, today as we look at your redemption plan for us, may we find hope in the midst of our difficult circumstances.

71
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:10,840
For your glory and for our good, in Jesus' name I pray, amen.

72
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:14,780
We're going to be in Luke chapter 24 in a little bit.

73
00:06:14,780 --> 00:06:19,540
It's going to take us a while to get there, but if you're new to the Bible, then you've got plenty of time to find it.

74
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:21,980
It's towards the back in the New Testament.

75
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:25,780
So, I'm going to ask you a question.

76
00:06:25,780 --> 00:06:28,620
I'm going to ask you to participate, but I already know what the answer is going to be.

77
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:29,960
Okay?

78
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:32,020
I'm a prophet, some might say.

79
00:06:32,020 --> 00:06:33,060
Just kidding.

80
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:38,400
Raise your hand if you've ever been disappointed by unmet expectations.

81
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:42,700
Pretty much all of us, right?

82
00:06:42,700 --> 00:06:47,660
I think the more accurate way to ask that question to get a little bit more diversified response would be,

83
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:50,960
how many of you have been disappointed by unmet expectations this morning?

84
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:55,680
Maybe a few less of us would raise our hands.

85
00:06:55,680 --> 00:07:02,500
I want to tell you a brief story about a time where I had some disappointment based on some unmet expectations.

86
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:07,900
A few friends of ours invited Marissa and I to go to an animal shelter with them.

87
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:10,260
They were going to be meeting the dog that they were adopting.

88
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:12,440
And so we said, that would be fun.

89
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:13,060
Let's do that.

90
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:13,760
We like dogs.

91
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:14,680
This will be great.

92
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:18,000
And so we went with them with no expectation.

93
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,500
We were just going to see their dog and celebrate with them.

94
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:25,640
And somehow we got conned into like meeting a dog that needed to be adopted.

95
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:37,740
And so for a week, Marissa and I, after meeting this lovely sweet puppy, wrestled with whether or not we should get a dog or not.

96
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:40,680
I think we have a picture of this lovely sweet puppy maybe.

97
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:51,320
So for this next week, we were debating about whether or not we should adopt Zoe.

98
00:07:51,320 --> 00:08:05,560
And the whole week, my mind was filled with expectations of my new best friend, of unconditional love and affection, of all upside and no downside.

99
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:16,080
And believe it or not, within less than 12 hours of Zoe being in our house, my expectations were dashed.

100
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:27,500
As I was trying to fall asleep on the floor beside the crate of an inconsolable dog, listening to my wife who had to get up at 5.30 to go work, yell at me, whose idea was this?

101
00:08:29,660 --> 00:08:42,700
Over the next few weeks, months, and years, this precious, beautiful dog would destroy a tremendous amount of my personal property and ruin a not so insignificant amount of my days.

102
00:08:42,700 --> 00:08:50,040
I can confidently say the bumper sticker that says my dog rescued me is full of baloney.

103
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:53,140
I rescued her.

104
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:58,140
And if she was a human, we would have multiple civil litigations against her.

105
00:08:58,140 --> 00:09:10,640
I quickly moved from excitement and anticipation to disappointment and coping with the new reality of being what millennials call a dog dad.

106
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:12,260
Right?

107
00:09:12,260 --> 00:09:13,640
This is my new reality.

108
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:18,440
How do I live with this thing that literally is out to destroy my house and herself?

109
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:21,100
Not because she's bad, just because she's nuts.

110
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:35,420
Now, I love her very much, and this is a bit of a lighthearted illustration, but if I knew you all better, I could tell you deep stories of disappointment in my life.

111
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:41,720
Where best friends betrayed me and the intended trajectory of my life was shattered in a weekend.

112
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:52,500
And I know if some of you knew me better, you would probably share stories of deep disappointment tied to unmet expectations.

113
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:58,720
Marriages that you expected that would last a lifetime ended abruptly.

114
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:09,340
The phone call you received one regular Saturday morning thinking it was a friend just checking in on you, giving you the news that a loved one had been lost too soon.

115
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:13,380
The 10th negative pregnancy test this year.

116
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:18,980
The basic physical that turned into a long-term terminal illness.

117
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:30,120
The person you love the most in life walking down a destructive path that is destroying them and impacting your relationship with them.

118
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:38,260
The unexpected and emotional challenge of aging in a fast-paced world that tends to ignore and marginalize those of the older generation.

119
00:10:38,260 --> 00:10:49,140
See, I don't have to know all of you all that well to know that we have sat in and felt the pain and disappointment of unmet expectations.

120
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:53,600
Some of you might even be in the room angry with me saying disappointment isn't even the right word.

121
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:55,380
Ryan, it's more like despair.

122
00:10:55,380 --> 00:11:06,380
So I don't know where you're at in your experience with disappointment, but I know that you can resonate with some of this.

123
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:14,100
And as we look to our passage this morning, what we're going to see is we're going to see the early followers of Jesus having this very same experience.

124
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:20,500
Day one, the crucifixion, Good Friday, the disappointment of unmet expectations.

125
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:24,020
Christ going to the cross is not what they had anticipated.

126
00:11:24,020 --> 00:11:27,140
It's not what they had envisioned for their lives to be.

127
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:29,680
Day two is silent.

128
00:11:31,740 --> 00:11:32,940
Not knowing what to do.

129
00:11:32,940 --> 00:11:45,200
So, we're going to go through these three days and unpack the emotions that the early disciples were feeling and think about how that might impact our lives.

130
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:48,560
Day one.

131
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:55,920
The early followers had given up their entire lives to follow Jesus, believing that he was the Savior, the Messiah of Israel.

132
00:11:55,920 --> 00:12:00,220
They staked their reputation and their future on his teachings and his miracles.

133
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:08,580
Everything they had was riding on Jesus, delivering them from the oppression of Rome and giving them lasting peace and victory.

134
00:12:08,580 --> 00:12:10,740
And rightfully so, right?

135
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:15,840
I mean, they watched him feed 5,000 plus people with a little boy's lunchbox.

136
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:19,500
They witnessed blind regain sight, the lame walk.

137
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:26,540
They watched skin diseases be healed miraculously and Lazarus raised from the dead.

138
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:31,800
That if he can do all of this, surely he could save the people from the oppression of Rome.

139
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:39,440
All of this culminated last Sunday at Corey's message on Palm Sunday, where Jesus has a triumphal entry into the city of Jerusalem.

140
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:44,200
Where there's crowds of people gathering together saying, Messiah, save now.

141
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:48,160
These early followers thought everything was going according to plan.

142
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:50,500
He is the Messiah.

143
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:51,720
He will save now.

144
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:53,180
This is the time.

145
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:54,920
This is the place.

146
00:12:54,920 --> 00:13:00,040
Everything is going according to plan until Thursday night.

147
00:13:00,040 --> 00:13:13,220
Chief priests and scribes, because of the betrayal of a close friend, Judas, take him away, put him through an illegal trial at night with shady witnesses and hand him over to Roman authorities.

148
00:13:13,220 --> 00:13:31,200
And instead of calling down power from heaven, which he could have done and the disciples probably expected him to do, he silently went like a lamb to the slaughter.

149
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:41,840
In less than a week, these early followers of Jesus went from eager anticipation to the overwhelming disappointment of unmet expectations.

150
00:13:41,840 --> 00:13:46,140
Can any of us resonate with this feeling?

151
00:13:46,140 --> 00:13:52,160
Listen to how one of the, two of the early followers say it in Luke chapter 24.

152
00:13:53,260 --> 00:13:58,480
They're actually talking to Jesus and they don't know it yet, but they say this, but we had hoped.

153
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:00,520
Look at your neighbor and say, had hoped.

154
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:08,020
We had hoped that he would be the one to redeem Israel.

155
00:14:08,020 --> 00:14:12,200
How many of us have stories that go that way?

156
00:14:12,200 --> 00:14:14,600
We had hoped.

157
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:17,340
I had hoped.

158
00:14:17,940 --> 00:14:22,380
I thought my life was going to go a certain direction and it went another direction.

159
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:27,200
The realities of living in a broken world have all hit us on one level or another.

160
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:33,460
And if we're honest, many of us end up resenting or blaming God altogether when disappointment hits.

161
00:14:33,460 --> 00:14:38,940
We wrestle with this tension of how could this have happened?

162
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:41,300
We had hoped that God would save our marriage.

163
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:47,320
We would hope that God would allow us to get pregnant or heal our friend's cancer or save our child from depression.

164
00:14:47,320 --> 00:14:50,880
And on and on it goes, we had hoped.

165
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:58,060
And if God is good and loving and powerful, then why did he allow all of these things to happen?

166
00:14:58,060 --> 00:15:03,400
And if he could keep these things from happening, but doesn't, then why should I trust him?

167
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:10,140
Anybody else resonate with the emotions of the early followers of Jesus?

168
00:15:10,140 --> 00:15:14,500
These are the questions that they would begin to wrestle with.

169
00:15:15,380 --> 00:15:24,500
Day one, the pain, the sorrow, the sadness, the disappointment of unmet expectation leads to what all of us who have had day one experiences know.

170
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:30,460
What comes next in day two is confusion and learning how to cope with the new reality of life.

171
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:34,420
How could this have happened?

172
00:15:34,420 --> 00:15:37,320
And what am I supposed to do now?

173
00:15:37,320 --> 00:15:47,320
Although the Gospels are filled about verses about day one and about day three, day two is eerily silent.

174
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:50,160
That's why we call it Silent Saturday.

175
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:53,120
Our best guess is that the disciples went home.

176
00:15:53,120 --> 00:16:03,060
There's actually one verse that states clearly that those who wanted Jesus crucified, they went home beating their chests in celebration.

177
00:16:03,060 --> 00:16:07,020
And I was reading that this week and I was just thinking about March Madness.

178
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:10,940
I was thinking about a team going into halftime up 20, beating their chests.

179
00:16:10,940 --> 00:16:12,100
Like this is over.

180
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:12,840
It's done.

181
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:13,500
We did it.

182
00:16:13,540 --> 00:16:14,320
We did the thing.

183
00:16:14,320 --> 00:16:19,640
This false teacher, this fake Messiah, he's gone.

184
00:16:19,640 --> 00:16:23,240
Little do they know the game's not over at halftime.

185
00:16:24,840 --> 00:16:35,160
So, these disappointed disciples in despair, they likely go home and it's Sabbath.

186
00:16:35,160 --> 00:16:38,660
Every good Jew does what on Sabbath?

187
00:16:38,660 --> 00:16:40,580
Rest.

188
00:16:40,580 --> 00:16:41,220
Nothing.

189
00:16:41,220 --> 00:16:46,540
A full day to sit and ask the questions.

190
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:49,100
To wrestle with where did we go wrong?

191
00:16:49,100 --> 00:16:51,860
How did we miss this?

192
00:16:54,840 --> 00:16:58,200
How could this have happened and what am I supposed to do now?

193
00:16:58,200 --> 00:17:10,560
My guess is that in a room this size, we've all come to our own disappointment and confusion and coping with some pretty creative strategies on how to cope with our disappointment and unmet expectations.

194
00:17:10,560 --> 00:17:15,860
We've rushed to replace lost relationships with new ones.

195
00:17:15,860 --> 00:17:22,160
We've found a unique cocktail of substances, hobbies, or entertainment to numb the pain.

196
00:17:23,020 --> 00:17:30,420
We've overly invested in our careers and productivity to try and manufacture the type of success that makes us forget the pain.

197
00:17:30,420 --> 00:17:34,140
Or, we've settled into our disappointment.

198
00:17:34,140 --> 00:17:38,340
We've become lonely, angry, and bitter.

199
00:17:38,340 --> 00:17:47,900
And we're unkind to others because we think if we can make other people experience the same pain that we have, then we won't be, at least we won't be alone.

200
00:17:50,620 --> 00:18:03,700
And it's in the shadow of day one disappointment and day two confusion and coping that the gospel writers, they introduce for us an alternative possibility to coping with the confusion and disappointment of our pain.

201
00:18:03,700 --> 00:18:12,400
An alternative solution to filling our souls, our hearts with the worldly pleasures.

202
00:18:13,500 --> 00:18:23,540
With the things that can never fill us and can never satisfy us, the gospel writers introduce what I would call day three hope.

203
00:18:23,540 --> 00:18:25,860
Look at your neighbor and say, hope.

204
00:18:25,860 --> 00:18:27,500
Hope.

205
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:38,720
This is the solution that Jesus meets us with in the midst of our despair, in the midst of our confusion, in the midst of our, I had hoped my life would go a certain way.

206
00:18:39,420 --> 00:18:41,980
Luke, chapter 24, starting in verse one.

207
00:18:41,980 --> 00:18:48,080
But on the first day, which is confusing because now it's our third day.

208
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:48,500
Got it?

209
00:18:48,500 --> 00:18:49,560
Day three.

210
00:18:49,560 --> 00:18:51,260
Luke says first day.

211
00:18:51,260 --> 00:18:52,400
I'm not a heretic, I promise.

212
00:18:52,400 --> 00:19:03,120
On the first day of the week at early dawn, they went to the tomb, taking the spices that they had prepared, and they found the stone rolled away from the tomb.

213
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:06,980
But when they went in, they did not find the body of the Lord Jesus.

214
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:14,380
While they were perplexed, confused about this, behold, two men stood by them in dazzling apparel.

215
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:21,920
And as they were frightened and bowed their faces to the ground, the mid said to them, why do you seek the living among the dead?

216
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:23,220
He is not here.

217
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:24,520
He has risen.

218
00:19:24,520 --> 00:19:35,980
And remember how he told you while he was still in Galilee that the Son of Man must be delivered into the hands of sinful men and be crucified on the third day and rise.

219
00:19:37,680 --> 00:19:38,760
Listen to this.

220
00:19:38,760 --> 00:19:42,980
And they remembered his words.

221
